// (1) out_or_wrap: Each bit of this output vector should indicate the logical OR between the corresponding input bit and its neighbour to the left. For example, out_or_wrap[3] should be the OR of in[3] and in[4]. For this part, treat the vector as wrapping around, so in[8]'s neighbour to the left is in[0].
// (2) out_xor: Each bit of this output vector should indicate the XOR between the corresponding input bit and its neighbour to the left. For example, out_xor[5] should be the XOR of in[5] and in[6]. Since in[8] has no neighbour to the left, out_xor[8] is not needed.
// (3) out_nand: Each bit of this output vector should indicate the logical NAND between the corresponding input bit and its neighbour to the right. For example, out_nand[2] should be the NAND of in[2] and in[1]. Since in[0] has no neighbour to the right, out_nand[0] is not needed.

