Implement a circuit that converts a 32-bit IEEE 754 floating-point number to a 10-bit unsigned integer. The output, y, is determined by the sign bit of the input x; if the sign bit is 1 (indicating a negative number), y is set to maximum (10'b1111111111). Otherwise, y is formed by combining the biased exponent (with an offset of 127) and the fractional part of the input, effectively performing a conversion from floating-point to an unsigned integer representation.
